# SABOTAGE: T162 / ADR-039 §3.3 — the run-definition PIN is stamped, stored, and
# then ignored at execution. `DispatchWorker` still snapshots the definition at
# enqueue and `RunRecord` still writes it onto the Run row, so every artifact a
# reviewer inspects looks correct: the job args carry a definition, the Run row
# carries a definition and a digest, the operator health view shows a pin. Only the
# ONE line that decides what actually runs goes back to the live workflow row.
#
# This is the most plausible form of the regression, because it is what a
# well-meaning refactor produces: someone "simplifies" the resolve step to derive the
# definition from the `wf` already in scope, the suite's pin-shaped assertions about
# the ROW still pass, and the pin silently becomes decoration. The whole point of
# T162 is that a tenant edit landing between enqueue and execution — or between Oban
# retries of one job — cannot change what an already-triggered run executes; under
# this patch it changes it again, and the stored digest now LIES about what ran.
#
# Flips the named execution reds: the edit-after-enqueue red (v2 runs instead of the
# pinned v1), its condition twin (the narrowed gate re-gates a queued run into a
# skip), and the retry red (attempt 2 executes the edited definition).
#
# Everything else stays GREEN, which is the claim's sharp edge: RED 2's three
# kill-switch/owner controls still skip (they were always live-read and are untouched
# here), RED 3/RED 3b still pass because the ROW and the ARGS still carry the correct
# v1 pin — proving the flip is about EXECUTION, not about the plumbing that records
# it, and that a gate resting only on the recorded pin would be vacuous.
#
# NOT TOUCHED, deliberately: `Samen.Automation.Definition` itself (the snapshot and
# the digest stay honest), `DispatchWorker`'s enqueue-time stamp, `RunRecord.open!/2`,
# and every live-read guard in `execute/4`.
#
# Substitutes one expression for another of the same shape, so nothing goes unused
# and the file still compiles under `--warnings-as-errors`.
# APP: samen_core
# TEST_FILES: test/automation/run_definition_pin_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: a workflow edited AFTER enqueue still executes the pinned v1 definition
# MUST_FAIL: a CONDITION edited after enqueue does not re-gate the queued run
# MUST_FAIL: a retry of the same job re-executes the pinned v1, not the edited v2
diff --git a/samen_core/lib/samen/automation/run_worker.ex b/samen_core/lib/samen/automation/run_worker.ex
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/automation/run_worker.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/automation/run_worker.ex
@@ -75,11 +75,11 @@ defmodule Samen.Automation.RunWorker do
   defp run(workflow_mod, args) do
     case load_workflow(workflow_mod, args["workflow_id"]) do
       {:ok, wf} ->
-        # T162. The workflow row is still loaded — but ONLY for the live checks
-        # (kill-switch, owner, org). What this run EXECUTES is the definition
-        # pinned into the job args at enqueue, so an edit landing in the queue (or
-        # between retries of this same job) cannot change it.
-        {_source, definition} = Definition.resolve(args, wf)
+        # SABOTAGE (T162/ADR-039 §3.3): the pin in the job args is ignored and the
+        # definition is taken from the workflow row AS IT LOOKS NOW. The Run row
+        # still records the pin (RunRecord reads the args), so the log claims one
+        # definition ran while another did.
+        {_source, definition} = {:pinned, Definition.snapshot(wf)}

         run_row = RunRecord.open!(wf, args)
         result = execute(wf, definition, args, run_row)
